<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <!--
      Deliberately OFF. The DirectML provider package lags the base package by
      a minor version, so it pulls a different Microsoft.Extensions.AI than
      Microsoft.Agents.AI does. Transitive pinning turns that ordinary
      resolution into a hard restore failure. Leave it off until the graph is
      known-good, then consider switching it on.
    -->
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>false</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    ============================================================================
    EXECUTION PROVIDER — DirectML.
    Target machine: 8C/16T Ryzen-class, 16 GB DDR4-2667, NVIDIA GTX (4 GB VRAM),
    AMD Radeon integrated, DirectX 12 FL 12.1, driver 32.0.15.6081 (2024-07-30).

    Why DirectML over CUDA on this box:
      - The 2024 driver predates current CUDA EP builds; no toolkit install needed.
      - DirectML spills to shared GPU memory (7.7 GB here) when 4 GB VRAM fills.
        CUDA hard-OOMs instead. With 4 GB that headroom matters.

    VERSION NOTE — verified nuget.org 19 Aug 2026:
      Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntimeGenAI          0.15.2   (base, CPU)
      Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntimeGenAI.DirectML 0.14.1   <- one minor BEHIND
      Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntimeGenAI.Cuda     0.13.2
    Provider packages trail the base package. Do not assume they match.
    0.14.1 is still post-0.6, so the AppendTokenSequences API and the in-box
    OnnxRuntimeGenAIChatClient are both present.
    ============================================================================
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Edge inference — DirectML">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntimeGenAI.DirectML" Version="0.14.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Agent orchestration — GA since 1.0 on 2026-04-02">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Agents.AI" Version="1.17.0" />
    <!--
      Microsoft.Extensions.AI is deliberately unpinned - it arrives transitively
      via Microsoft.Agents.AI and OnnxRuntimeGenAI.Managed. If restore complains
      about a missing version for a direct reference, pin at the version NuGet
      actually resolved rather than guessing.
    -->
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Data">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Data.Sqlite" Version="9.0.8" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ============================================================================
    Never install two OnnxRuntimeGenAI provider packages at once. The resulting
    DllNotFoundException names neither package. Verify with:
        dotnet list package include-transitive | findstr OnnxRuntimeGenAI

    Model folder MUST match the provider:
      DirectML / CUDA  ->  gpu/gpu-int4-rtn-block-32          (Phi-4-mini)
      CPU              ->  cpu_and_mobile/cpu-int4-rtn-block-32-acc-level-4
    A CPU model folder loads fine under the DirectML package and silently runs
    on the CPU. Edge.Doctor check 2 exists to catch exactly that.

    FALLBACK to CPU (swap the ItemGroup above):
      <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntimeGenAI" Version="0.15.2" />

    OPTIONAL Semantic Kernel path — both still -alpha as of 19 Aug 2026:
      <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.SemanticKernel" Version="1.77.0" />
      <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.SemanticKernel.Connectors.Onnx" Version="1.77.0-alpha" />
      <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.SemanticKernel.Agents.Core" Version="1.77.0-alpha" />
    ============================================================================
  -->
</Project>
